      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), we need to analyze the standard form of the equation of a hyperbola and identify its key parameters.\n\nA hyperbola in standard form is typically written as \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\) (horizontal transverse axis) or \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\) (vertical transverse axis).\n\nFor the given equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), it is a hyperbola with a horizontal transverse axis because the term involving \\(x^2\\) is positive.\n\nHere, \\(a^2 = 99\\) and \\(b^2 = 36\\). Therefore, \\(a = \\sqrt{99} = 3\\sqrt{11}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{36} = 6\\).\n\nThe distance between the vertices of a hyperbola along its transverse axis is \\(2a\\). Here, \\(a = 3\\sqrt{11}\\), so the distance between the vertices is \\(2 \\times 3\\sqrt{11} = 6\\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code using sympy to confirm our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na = sp.sqrt(99)\nb = sp.sqrt(36)\ndistance_between_vertices = 2 * a\n\nprint(distance_between_vertices)\n```\n```output\n6*sqrt(11)\n```\nThe distance between the vertices of the hyperbola \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\) is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{11}}\\).",
